AB  - Paragangliomas, although well described in the adult population, the diagnosis and treatment of these infrequent neoplasms remains poorly characterised in children. In this chapter we describe and review the clinical presentation, genetics, biochemistry, imaging and treatment of children with benign or malignant paragangliomas. Paediatric paragangliomas are more frequently bilateral, familial, multifocal and malignant compared to adults. In approximately half of paediatric paragangliomas, there is a mutation of one of the known susceptibility genes, Von Hippel-Lindau disease being the commonest genetic disorder. The majority present with hypertension due to catecholamine hypersecretion and are diagnosed by assessment of plasma and/or urine metanephrines and cross-sectional radiological imaging, usually magnetic resonance imaging. Complete surgical removal is the treatment of choice, but for metastatic disease MIBG therapy may be effective either alone or in association with chemotherapy. In all cases genetic causes should be sought. © 2022, Springer Nature Switzerland AG.
