AB  - Improved understanding of several unique imaging characteristics of peripheral nerves has led to steady progress in producing tissue-selective images in a technique called magnetic resonance neurography (MRN). These images have dramatically advanced the diagnostic methodology for evaluating peripheral nerve disorders, providing far greater specificity and spatial accuracy than the longstanding practice of evaluation limited to physical examination and electrodiagnostics. Advances in MRN have led to the generation of three-dimensional pure nerve images that not only depict alterations in nerve caliber and location that are caused by impingements, but also demonstrate an array of pathologic detail that can distinguish a variety of medical etiologies from surgically treatable etiologies. At the same time, MRN has revolutionized the spatial accuracy and detection threshold for disorders that have defied diagnostic differentiation for many decades. In this sense, image diagnostics for peripheral nerve disorders has finally reached a level of detail and sophistication that neurosurgeons have been accustomed to for brain imaging. Conditions such as neurogenic thoracic outlet syndrome, pudendal neuralgia, and piriformis syndrome can now be reliably diagnosed by imaging, so it is now possible to confidently distinguish spinal from peripheral causes of pain, weakness, and numbness affecting the face arms, legs, chest, and pelvis at centers where neuroradiologists are capable and trained. Additionally, focal and systemic neuropathies including those associated with diabetes and autoimmune disorders are evaluable. Additionally, MRN can track degeneration and follow the progress of regeneration. Advanced imaging of spinal nerve roots can rule in or rule out foraminal pathology based on pathologic effects in the neural tissue. It is critical for neurosurgeons to be aware of these technologies to direct neuroradiologists to provide high-quality image diagnostics in this area. © 2023 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
