AB  - Diagnostic imaging of peripheral nerves has advanced dramatically with the introduction of new imaging techniques such as T2 weighted Magnetic Resonance Neurography, Diffusion Neurography, and Diffusion Tensor Imaging of peripheral nerve. These techniques can precise a high level of critical diagnostic information as to nerve compression, nerve irritation, degeneration and regeneration. Neurography imaging of spinal roots and proximal spinal nerves is useful for spinal diagnostics. Neurogenic Thoracic Outlet syndrome can be diagnosed objectively and reliably with neurography. In the pelvis, conditions such as piriformis syndrome and pudendal neuralgia are now also subject to clinically reliable image diagnosis. Other peripheral problems such as obstetrical brachial plexus palsy, and schwannomas, as well as various targets for MRI guided injections can be usefully characterized with these imaging techniques. In the setting of traumatic nerve injury and surgical repair, neurography can be used to assess the degree of injury and to monitor the progress of nerve regeneration after repair. © 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
