AB  - Direct imaging of the peripheral nervous system is an integral part in the diagnostic workup of neuromuscular diseases. It can also be used to guide diagnostic and therapeutic interventions such as biopsies or injections. The primary imaging modalities are MRI and ultrasound, whereas CT and conventional radiography are complementary modalities as they cannot depict nerves directly and yield inferior results regarding muscles. The strength of MRI lies in the superior soft tissue contrast. Preferentially, a high field strength (3 Tesla) is used to assess peripheral nerves. New techniques and sequences such as diffusion tensor imaging will also enhance the spectrum of application. Depending on the body region different coils are applied, which limits the size and the number of regions which can be examined in one MRI examination. In MRI examinations of the neuromuscular system, safety measures are the same as for all other MRI studies. High resolution ultrasound can visualize peripheral nerves with superior spatial and temporal resolution in comparison to MRI. In an ultrasound examination, the scanning region can be extended in a swift sweep, for example, from the hand to the neck. Deep lying nerves as the sciatic nerve can only be visualized with less resolution due to the lower transducer frequency. Also, bones and gas (e.g., in the intestines) block the view with ultrasound. Safety concerns regarding ultrasound rarely interfere with daily clinical routine practices. Both modalities, MRI and ultrasound, are constantly evolving and changing as new techniques and examination protocols are developed. Collectively, imaging offers exciting new diagnostic and therapeutic possibilities that will continue to advance the field of neuromuscular neurology. © Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2021.
