AB  - The nervous system is a complex anatomical network of interconnected neurons. The spinal cord and brain are its core elements and are protected by overlying bone. The presence of bone makes access and imaging of the spinal cord and brain challenging using noninvasive methods. Here, we review optical imaging techniques (two-photon microscopy, bioluminescence) that can be applied in vivo to visualize nervous tissue by removing bone as a barrier for light penetration, as well as other techniques, such as magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), computed tomography (CT), positron emission tomography (PET), single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT), magnetoencephalography (MEG), and electroencephalogram (EEG), that can image superficial and/or deeply seated tissue with an intact skull and spine. Ultrasound (US) is mainly applied to the peripheral nervous system, but is making advances in brain and spine applications, especially by opening up the blood-brain barrier to deliver therapeutic drugs and contrast materials. Imaging of the nervous system encompasses anatomical, perfusion, diffusion, biochemical, molecular, and functional aspects. Choice of imaging techniques is guided by which aspects of the nervous system is investigated, as well as its spatial resolution and time scale. We here review these aspects to determine the selection of a particular imaging technique and its application to different aspects of neural and tissue engineering. © 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights are reserved including those for text and data mining AI training and similar technologies.
