AB  - Iatrogenic nerve injury constitutes a frequent and often complex clinical challenge for peripheral nerve surgeons. Injuries may include direct intraoperative nerve injury, such as from transection or misplaced retractor, nerve injury from patient positioning, or postoperative complications arising from recurrent entrapment, scar tethering, or others. Pain arising from these injuries differs from general or postoperative pain problems, often creating neuropathic pain from the site of injury and neuralgic pain in the distribution of the affected nerve, as well as neuroma pain, and deafferentation pain. Use of magnetic resonance neurography and diffusion tensor imaging can aid greatly in identifying, locating, and assessing the injuries. Advice given in past decades that reccomended delays before proceeding with nerve repair has now been updated generally in favor of early surgery as the first consideration. Delay in referral or treatment when an iatrogenic nerve injury has occurred is no longer warranted. Injuries that occur as a consequence of positioning or anesthesia, whether during operations for neurosurgery or other types of surgery, tend to be delayed in referral to a nerve specialist often—without benefit for the additional delay. Since there may be options for treatment that non-peripheral nerve surgeons are not aware of, it is incumbent for surgeons to recognize and pursue early evaluation and evaluation and treatment by subspecialists as a primary duty if they are not able to fully evaluate and repair the injury. Enthusiasm for new surgical techniques and anatomical approaches is often followed by appraisal of complications. In many circumstances, complications have been iatrogenic nerve injury, such as lateral approaches to the spine or laproscopic port sites. Care must be taken to reduce nerve injury risks when a new procedure or methodology leads to a new spike in the incidence of nerve injury. Awareness, mitigation, and technique refinement have become critical in avoiding iatrogenic nerve injuries in novel procedures. © 2023 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
