AB  - Introduction: MRI is increasingly used for the diagnosis of peripheral nerve pathologies and in particular for the study of the brachial plexus. Main messages: The used MRI protocol includes a bilateral and comparative study of the brachial plexus. It is based on a combination of T1- or T2-weighted sequences without fat saturation for an anatomical study in the sagittal and coronal planes of the brachial plexus and its environment, and T2-weighted sequences with fat saturation for intra-neural (T2 STIR coronal and sagittal with reformatting by MIP) and intramuscular (T2 Dixon or STIR axial) abnormality detection. Gadolinium administration is recommended for the study of inflammatory or tumoral plexopathies. An additional high-resolution sequence is performed for the study of traumatic proximal lesions. Conclusion: MRI is a powerful imaging modality for brachial plexus exploration. It combines high-resolution and high-contrast sequences. © 2019 Société française de radiologie
