AD  - General Electric Global Research Center, Schenectady, NY, United States
AB  - Whole-body magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) was introduced into medical practice in the early 1980s. It has become a standard medical procedure producing high-quality noninvasive images of the internal human anatomy without the use of X-rays or radioactive isotopes. It is estimated that there have been more than 600 000 000 clinical studies since the introduction of the technique and projected that more than 80 000 000 clinical studies will be performed in 2012. These studies have been performed with an excellent safety record even though intense static and very rapidly switched gradient magnetic fields are utilized. Modern developments in MRI involve the use of ever stronger magnets and gradient fields requiring continual attention to safety considerations. Despite the excellent MRI safety record, scrupulous attention is required to the exclusion of magnetic materials, either in the vicinity of scanners or within the bodies of patients, as these constitute potentially lethal risks. The increasing use of implanted active devices (e.g., cardiac defibrillators) also raises new safety concerns. Although not specifically safety issues, a number of low-level sensory effects can be produced by MRI scanning and these should be considered when very strong magnetic fields or very rapidly switched gradients are employed. The semicircular canals of the inner ear appear to be especially sensitive to head motion within strong magnetic fields. This is linked to field-induced eye motions (magnetic nystagmus) and the sensation of vertigo. At high field strengths, a novel form of motion sickness, here termed magnet sickness, may appear in susceptible individuals. © 2013 John Wiley & Sons, Ltd.
