AB  - Medical imaging techniques such as magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), computed tomography (CT), and ultrasound are ideal tools for visualizing anatomical structures. Each technique has its own advantages in medical imaging. MRI is the most widely used technique in clinical and research fields especially for observing peripheral nerve tissue. The MRI equipment normally uses a magnetic field from 1.5 to 7.0 T in nerve tissue engineering-related research. The 7.0-T MRI equipment has excellent resolution to visualize the thin regenerated nerve fibers of rats in vivo. High resolution of 7-T MRI is used to track nerve tissues and the transplanted cells labeled with superparamagnetic materials. To obtain information about nerve sprouting, MR images are acquired periodically after the surgery. Nerve tissue images in different planes in MRI can be shown by sagittal, coronal, or axial views. It is possible to obtain the images simply by the appropriate assignment of the gradient coils. While imaging has become more and more useful in evaluating the soft tissue, it is also more complicated because of the increasing capabilities of MRI to obtain various imaging information for the targeted tissue. MR diffusion tensor imaging, tractography, and other novel imaging tools and contrast agents will be beneficial to imaging of nerve tissue engineering. In order to extract information obtained from the images of the soft issues, special MRI sequences are necessary. New progresses and research in MRI are expected to be implemented in the near future in nerve tissue engineering and regeneration. © 2017 John Wiley & Sons, Inc.
