AD  - Department of Paediatrics, National University of Singapore, Singapore
AD  - Department of Paediatrics, Johns Hopkins Univ. Sch. of Medicine, United States
AD  - Department of Laboratory Medicine, Children's Medical Institute, National University Hospital, Singapore
AD  - Department of Neurology, National Neuroscience Institute, Singapore 308433, 11 Jalan Tan Tock Seng, Singapore
AB  - Introduction: The spinocerebellar ataxias are a rare group of inherited neurodegenerative disorders. Epilepsy has not previously been associated with spinocerebellar ataxia type 2 (SCA2). Clinical Picture: We describe a family with 3 affected members who had typical phenotypic and MRI features of SCA2. Two had focal epilepsy with complex partial seizures and epileptiform discharges on electroencephalography. Trinucleotide expansions in the pathological range were found in the SCA2 gene, confirming SCA2. Sequencing of the expanded SCA2 gene did not reveal any new mutations that could account for epilepsy. Treatment and Outcome: The focal epilepsy was well-controlled with carbamazepine. Conclusion: We hypothesise that the new feature of focal epilepsy is due to co-existence of a separate unlinked epilepsy susceptibility gene with the expanded SCA2 gene. Under this oligogenic model, both genes must be present, and co-inheritance of this susceptibility gene with the expanded SCA2 gene causes a complex interaction which triggers epilepsy.; Indications:1 patient with spinocerebellar ataxia type 2 with focal epilepsy.; Patients:One 52 year old Malay man. Patient dropped out due to side effects.; TypeofStudy:A case report describing a family with genetically proven spinocerebellar ataxia type 2 (SCA2) treated with Symmetrel with the previously undescribed feature of focal epilepsy treated with carbamazepine.; DosageDuration:Dosage and duration not stated.; Results:The patient was started on Symmetrel for ataxia. There was mild functional improvement, but it was stopped when he developed hallucinations and psychosis. He achieved good seizure control with slow-release carbamazepine. Both the index patient and his brother are still alive 2 years after the diagnosis, while the son has passed away from infected bedsores.; AdverseEffects:1 patient developed hallucinations and psychosis.; FreeText:The index patient presented with a 3-month history of complex partial seizures. The seizures consisted of blank stares, lasting for 2 to 3 minutes, during which he was unresponsive. These were sometimes associated with oral automatisms. The seizures became secondarily generalized on one occasion. He also complained of slow progressive gait unsteadiness for 5 years. His deceased mother, his brother and son also had a history of similar gait difficulties. His mother and brother had noticed gait difficulties in the fifth decade of life, while his son developed symptoms much earlier at age 13. On examination, the index patient was found to have marked limb and truncal ataxia and dysarthria. Ocular saccades were distinctly slow. Deep tendon reflexes were absent. His affected brother showed similar signs. His son was clinically more severely affected; he was dysarthric to the point of anarthria, wheelchair-bound and dependent in his activities of daily living, requiring nasogastric tubing. Thyroid function tests, vitamin E levels and ceruloplasmin and ceruloplasmin were normal. Nerve conduction studies showed axonal sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy. An electroencephalogram (EEG) of the index patient showed right temporal slowing and right fronto-temporal epileptiform activity. Genetic studies confirmed the diagnosis of SCA2 with 39 CGA repeats (normal range, 13 to 34) in the SCA2 gene. The brother and the son of the index patient were found to have 37 and 52 repeats respectively, confirming SCA2. An EEG of the affected son showed background slowing, bilateral independent temporal slowing and left temporal epileptiform activity. The presence of an abnormal background, and absence of centrotemporal, occipital or frontal sharp waves in the EEG made the differential diagnosis of idiopathic partial epilepsy of childhood lee likely in the son. The magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain of the father and son both showed cerebellar and brainstem atrophy.; AuthorsConclusions:In conclusion, we describe a family with confirmed SCA2 with the previously undescribed phenotypic feature of focal epilepsy, a feature which had previously been exclusive to SCA10 among all the SCAs. We believe this new feature reflects the influence of a co-existing epilepsy susceptibility gene on the expanded SCA2 gene. Future clinicians, when confronted with a patient with spinocerebellar ataxia and focal epilepsy, should consider both SCA10 and SCA2.
